    {
      "type": "thinking",
      "thinking": "Let me analyze this fragment carefully.\n\nThis is an OIML Certificate:\n- Number: R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01 revision 11\n- Project number: 3891281\n- Page total: 13\n- Member State: The Netherlands\n- Scheme: A\n- Issuing authority: NMi Certin B.V., OIML Issuing Authority NL1\n- Person responsible: M.Ph.D. Schmidt\n- Address: Thijsseweg 11, 2629 JA Delft, The Netherlands\n- Phone: +31 88 636 2332\n- Email: certin@nmi.nl\n- Website: www.nmi.nl\n- Date issued: 27 January 2025\n- Applicant and Manufacturer: ABB Limited, Oldends Lane, GL10 3TA Stonehouse, United Kingdom\n- Certified type: Electromagnetic water meter\n- Type designations: AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z and FET4*\n  - Actually \"Type: AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z and FET4 *\"\n- Recommendation: R 49-1 (2013) — \"Water meters intended for the metering of cold potable water and hot water\"\n- Accuracy class: 1 and 2\n\nTest reports:\n- NMi-2254799-01 dated 14 August 2020, 235 pages\n- NMi-2254799-03 dated 30 September 2020, 44 pages\n- NMi-2254799-04 dated 24 December 2020, 180 pages\n- NMi-2564429-01 dated 2 August 2021, 26 pages\n- NMi-2496280-01 dated 26 August 2021, 29 pages\n- NMi-2496280-02 dated 23 November 2021, 25 pages\n- NMi-2563125-01 dated 22 September 2023, 94 pages\n\nDocumentation folder no. T11851-8 — that's a documentation_file in test_reports? The schema says role: type_evaluation_report | test_report | documentation_file | evaluation_report. The documentation folder no. T11851-8 can be a documentation_file.\n\nNow the type designations. The certified type: \"An electromagnetic water meter, Type: AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z and FET4*\"\n\nWith wildcards:\n- X being 1 for standard or 3 for advanced or 5 for premium (mobile Comms - MC) variant\n- Y being 1, 2, 8 or 9 (integral/remote version, optionally sensor only)\n- Z being R (reduced bore), V (virtual full bore) or F (full bore)\n\nSo type designations: [\"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\", \"FET4*\"] or list concrete: FEW41Y.Z, FEW43Y.Z, FEW45Y.Z, FET4... Hmm. The type designations mentioned: \"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\" and \"FET4*\". Tables mention FEW41Y.Z, FEW43Y.Z, FEW45Y.Z. I'll include the explicit designations: \"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\", \"FET4*\", plus maybe \"FEW41Y.Z\", \"FEW43Y.Z\", \"FEW45Y.Z\". The schema says \"all type/model identifiers mentioned\". So include all: AquaMaster4, FEW4XY.Z, FET4, FEW41Y.Z, FEW43Y.Z, FEW45Y.Z. Hmm, better to be careful. I'll list: [\"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\", \"FET4*\", \"FEW41Y.Z\", \"FEW43Y.Z\", \"FEW45Y.Z\"].\n\nModel family: The certificate covers a family of models. The model variants are defined by X, Y, Z codes. There are tables 5-12 that break down by bore (reduced/virtual full/full) and power (AC/battery). The fragment includes Tables 1 and 2 which are general characteristics for transmitter types FEW41Y.Z & FEW43Y.Z (Table 1) and FEW45Y.Z (Table 2).\n\nFor model_family, I could include models based on the family definition: X ∈ {1, 3, 5}, Y ∈ {1, 2, 8, 9}, Z ∈ {R, V, F}. But the fragment doesn't enumerate concrete models beyond the wildcards. Hmm. Table 1 covers FEW41Y.Z and FEW43Y.Z; Table 2 covers FEW45Y.Z. I think a reasonable model_family:\n\n- family_name: \"AquaMaster4\"\n- models: entries for FEW41Y.Z, FEW43Y.Z, FEW45Y.Z? But Y and Z are still wildcards...\n\nGiven the rules: \"model_family: include ONLY if cert covers >1 model variant\". The cert covers multiple variants. The model variants in this fragment are characterized by transmitter type (FEW41Y.Z/FEW43Y.Z vs FEW45Y.Z) and later by bore/power in tables 5-12 (not in this fragment... wait, tables 5-12 are listed in the table of contents on page 2 but their content is not in this fragment; the fragment ends at \"Table 3 Software identification...\").\n\nSo this fragment contains Tables 1 and 2 content. I should extract characteristics from Tables 1 and 2.\n\nTable 1 (FEW41Y.Z and FEW43Y.Z):\n- Measuring principle: Electromagnetic\n- Accuracy class: 1 and 2\n- Environmental class: O (installed outdoors)\n- Electromagnetic environment: E2\n- Mechanical class: M1\n- Temperature range ambient: -25 °C / +55 °C (for both flow sensor and flow transmitter)\n- Water temperature class: T50 (+0,1 °C / +50 °C)\n- Maximum admissible pressure (MAP): 1,6 MPa (16 bar)\n- Orientation: All positions\n- Flow profile sensitivity class: U0 and D0, except DN250+ which have U3 and D0\n- Reverse flow: sensor intended to measure reverse flow\n- Pressure loss class: varies by size — reduced bore DN40/DN50: Δp40 (0.40 bar); >DN50: Δp63 (0.63 bar); full bore DN40/DN50: Δp10 (0.10 bar); DN65-DN200: Δp16 (0.16 bar); DN250+: Δp10 (0.10 bar)\n- Power supply: AC mains 95-240 VAC @ 50/60 Hz; renewable energy DC 6-32 V; replaceable battery 2x 3.6 V (operating range 2.9-3.6 V)\n- Software identification: see table 3\n- Checking facilities: Type P (permanent) automatic checking facility\n\nTable 2 (FEW45Y.Z): same except:\n- Electromagnetic environment: E1 for transmitter with external antenna cable; E2 without\n- Power supply: AC mains 95-240 VAC @ 50/60 Hz including 4x 3.6 V replaceable batteries; renewable 6-32 V including 4x 3.6 V batteries; replaceable battery 6x 3.6 V (range 2.9-3.6 V)\n\nNow how to structure. The two tables differ per transmitter-type group. I could treat this as model_level where model = \"FEW41Y.Z/FEW43Y.Z\" and \"FEW45Y.Z\". Attributes that vary: electromagnetic_environment, power_supply_battery_count (2x vs 6x), battery configuration.\n\nAlternatively, put shared characteristics at type_level and differing ones at model_level.\n\nPer Rule 4, matrix detection: attributes varying per model → model_level. Models here: \"FEW41Y.Z/FEW43Y.Z\" and \"FEW45Y.Z\". Hmm, but FEW41Y.Z and FEW43Y.Z are grouped in one table, and Y/Z wildcards remain.\n\nI think a reasonable approach:\n\nmodel_family:\n  family_name: \"AquaMaster4\"\n  models:\n    - model_id: \"FEW41Y.Z\", label: \"standard variant\" (X=1)\n    - model_id: \"FEW43Y.Z\", label: \"advanced variant\" (X=3)\n    - model_id: \"FEW45Y.Z\", label: \"premium (mobile Comms - MC) variant\" (X=5)\n    - model_id: \"FET4*\" (sensor only)\n\nActually wait — Y being 1,2,8,9 (integral/remote version, optionally sensor only) and Z being R/V/F. FET4 is likely the sensor-only type. The type designation pattern FEW4XY.Z covers the transmitters. FET4* might be the flow sensor. Actually FET4 is the flow sensor tube (ABB AquaMaster FET4 flowtube). So components: flow sensor FET4 and transmitter FEW4...? Hmm, that's plausible: AquaMaster4 consists of FET sensor + FEW transmitter. But the certificate doesn't explicitly say \"components\". It says \"Identification of the certified type: An electromagnetic water meter, Type: AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z and FET4*\". Tables 5-8 are \"family of instruments\" and 9-12 \"indicating device\" — so there are components: instrument family and indicating device. But content of tables 5-12 isn't in this fragment.\n\nI could add components:\n- flow sensor: FET4*\n- transmitter: FEW41Y.Z, FEW43Y.Z, FEW45Y.Z\n\nHmm, is that fabrication? The type designation line mentions both FEW4XY.Z and FET4. The tables refer to \"flow sensor and flow transmitter\". I think it's reasonable to record components with roles \"flow transmitter\" and \"flow sensor (flowtube)\". But rule 8 says don't fabricate. The mention \"for both flow sensor and flow transmitter\" supports that the instrument comprises a flow sensor and a transmitter. I'll include components with role \"transmitter\" (types FEW41Y.Z/FEW43Y.Z/FEW45Y.Z) and \"flow sensor\" (FET4). Actually let me be more conservative: I'll include them but keep roles descriptive.\n\nActually, since the wildcard explanation says Y being \"integral/remote version, optionally sensor only\" — the FEW transmitter can be integral or remote. The FET4 is the sensor. I'll include components.\n\nCharacteristics extraction:\n\ntype_level (shared across both tables):\n- measuring_principle: \"Electromagnetic\" (string value)\n- accuracy_class: hmm — this is at certificate level too. For characteristics, maybe \"1 and 2\" as string or list. I'll use value: \"1 and 2\"? Or value: [\"1\",\"2\"]. Schema says value: number | {min,max} | string. So string \"1 and 2\" is fine. Hmm, maybe \"1, 2\". I'll use \"1 and 2\".\n\nActually maybe better as accuracy_classes at recommendation level: [1, 2]. Yes — recommendation.accuracy_classes: [1, 2].\n\n- environmental_class: \"O (installed outdoors)\" — value string \"O\", with note? I could put value \"O\" and maybe a separate note. Keep value: \"O (installed outdoors)\". Hmm, canonical: value string. I'll use value: \"O (installed outdoors)\". Or value \"O\". Let me keep the descriptive part: value: \"O\", and maybe include qualifier. I think value: \"O (installed outdoors)\" is fine as a string.\n\nActually, cleaner: value: \"O\". But losing info. The rules allow string values. I'll do value: \"O (installed outdoors)\".\n\n- mechanical_class: \"M1\"\n- temperature_range_ambient: {min: -25, max: 55}, unit °C\n- water_temperature_class: T50 — value \"T50\"; water temperature range +0.1 to +50 °C. I could add water_temperature_range: {min: 0.1, max: 50}, unit °C. The original: \"T50 (+0,1 °C / +50 °C)\". So water_temperature_class: \"T50\" and water_temperature_range: {min: 0.1, max: 50} °C.\n- maximum_admissible_pressure: 1.6 MPa (16 bar). Map: value 1.6, unit \"MPa\". Could also record as bar 16. I'll record value 1.6 unit MPa. Maybe include secondary: I'll just do MPa.\n- orientation: \"All positions (horizontal, vertical, or diagonal)\" string.\n- reverse_flow: \"The sensor is intended to measure reverse flow\" — value string true-ish. I'll use value: \"intended to measure reverse flow\" or boolean? Value must be number|{min,max}|string. Use string. Maybe attribute reverse_flow_capability: \"measured\". Hmm, keep value: \"The sensor is intended to measure reverse flow\"? That's long. I'll simplify: value: \"intended to measure reverse flow\". Hmm, but don't fabricate... paraphrase is OK as long as faithful. I'll use \"intended to measure reverse flow\".\n- checking_facilities: \"Type P (permanent) automatic checking facility\" → value: \"Type P (permanent) automatic checking facility\" or \"Type P\". I'll use value: \"Type P (permanent) automatic checking facility\".\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class: \"U0 and D0 (0 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream)\" with exception for DN250+: \"U3 and D0\". This varies by configuration (size). Could be config_level with axis \"size\". Let me think: config_level entries: attribute flow_profile_sensitivity_class, axis \"nominal_size\", values: [{condition: \"DN < 250\", value: \"U0 and D0 (0 x DN upstream, 0 x DN downstream)\"}, {condition: \"DN ≥ 250\", value: \"U3 and D0 (3 x DN upstream, 0 x DN downstream)\"}]. The condition in values is {condition, value, unit_symbol}. value must be object with value field: {condition, value: \"...\", unit_symbol: null}. OK.\n\n- pressure_loss_class: varies by bore type and size. config_level: attribute pressure_loss_class, axis... two axes: bore (reduced vs (virtual) full) and size. Hmm. Maybe use matrix or config with condition combining: e.g. condition \"reduced bore, DN40-DN50\" value \"Δp40 (0.40 bar)\". Conditions as strings are allowed. Let me encode:\n  - {condition: \"reduced bore, DN40–DN50\", value: \"Δp 40\", unit \"bar\", value 0.40?} Hmm the value \"Δp 40 (0,40 bar)\" is class + value. I could record pressure_loss as numeric with class in another attribute. Simpler: value string \"Δp 40 (0.40 bar)\".\n\nLet me define config_level entries:\nattribute: pressure_loss, axis: \"bore_type_and_size\", values:\n- {condition: \"reduced bore, DN40 and DN50\", value: {\"value\": \"Δp 40\", ...}} — wait structure: {condition, value, unit_symbol} where value is number|string|{min,max}. So {condition: \"...\", value: \"Δp 40 (0.40 bar)\"}.\n\nAlternatively split numeric: attribute pressure_loss_max with value 0.40 bar and attribute pressure_loss_class \"Δp 40\". That's more structured. Let me do:\n\nconfig_level:\n1. attribute: pressure_loss_class, axis: \"bore_type_and_nominal_size\", values:\n   - {condition: \"reduced bore, DN40 and DN50\", value: \"Δp 40\"}\n   - {condition: \"reduced bore, larger than DN50\", value: \"Δp 63\"}\n   - {condition: \"virtual or full bore, DN40 and DN50\", value: \"Δp 10\"}\n   - {condition: \"virtual or full bore, DN65 to DN200\", value: \"Δp 16\"}\n   - {condition: \"virtual or full bore, DN250 and larger\", value: \"Δp 10\"}\n2. attribute: pressure_loss, axis same, values with numeric bar values:\n   - 0.40, 0.63, 0.10, 0.16, 0.10\n\nThat's decent. But maybe just combine into one to reduce noise. I'll do both class and value in one string? The rules prefer structured values but strings allowed for symbols/classes. I'll include pressure_loss numeric (bar) config-level and pressure_loss_class config-level. Hmm, that doubles entries. Alternatively a matrix_table \"Pressure loss class by bore type and size\". The tables 1/2 are inside <table> but as label-value rows. The pressure loss cell is a nested multi-line cell.\n\nI think config_level with numeric pressure_loss (unit bar) plus class in the condition/value string... Let me do attribute \"pressure_loss_class\" with value \"Δp 40 (0.40 bar)\" strings — keeps fidelity, single entry set. Hmm but then unit extraction rule violated (0.40 bar embedded). The rules say pull units out when the value is numeric. \"Δp 40\" is a class designation though. I'll split:\n\n- attribute: pressure_loss, axis: \"bore_type_and_size\", values: [{condition: \"reduced bore, DN40-DN50\", value: 0.4, unit_symbol: \"bar\"}, {condition: \"reduced bore, >DN50\", value: 0.63, unit_symbol: \"bar\"}, {condition: \"(virtual) full bore, DN40-DN50\", value: 0.1, unit_symbol: \"bar\"}, {condition: \"(virtual) full bore, DN65-DN200\", value: 0.16, unit_symbol: \"bar\"}, {condition: \"(virtual) full bore, DN250 and larger\", value: 0.1, unit_symbol: \"bar\"}]\n\n- and pressure_loss_class similar with values \"Δp 40\", \"Δp 63\", \"Δp 10\", \"Δp 16\", \"Δp 10\". Maybe skip class and keep only numeric? The class (Δp 40) is derived from bar value ×100. I'll include both to be faithful but it's a bit noisy. I'll include pressure_loss_class only, with combined string, actually... Let me decide: include both entries. It's fine.\n\nHmm, actually simpler and cleaner: one config_level entry per attribute:\n\nattribute: pressure_loss_class, axis: \"bore type and nominal size\", values: [{condition:\"reduced bore DN40 and DN50\", value:\"Δp 40 (0.40 bar)\"}, ...]. Single entry, full fidelity. The unit is embedded but value is a composite class string, acceptable. I'll go with this single approach to keep the output manageable... but rule 2 strictness says separate numeric from unit. For a class designation like \"Δp 40 (0,40 bar)\", it's a labeled class; I think numeric split is better for the number. Ugh.\n\nDecision: I'll emit two config_level attribute entries: pressure_loss_class (string \"Δp 40\" etc.) and maximum_pressure_loss (numeric bar). This follows the rules best.\n\n- Power supply: For Table 1: AC mains 95-240 VAC @ 50/60 Hz; renewable/DC 6-32 V; replaceable battery 2x 3.6 V (operating range 2.9-3.6 V).\n  type_level: \n  - power_supply_voltage_ac: {min: 95, max: 240} V (AC)\n  - power_supply_frequency: {min: 50, max: 60} Hz\n  - power_supply_voltage_dc: {min: 6, max: 32} V\n  - battery_voltage: 3.6 V\n  - battery_count: 2 (model_level: 2 vs 6)\n  - battery_operating_range: {min: 2.9, max: 3.6} V\n\n  The battery count differs between tables: Table 1: 2x 3.6 V; Table 2: 6x 3.6 V. So model_level: attribute battery_count, values: [{model: \"FEW41Y.Z/FEW43Y.Z\", value: 2}, {model: \"FEW45Y.Z\", value: 6}].\n\n  Also Table 2 AC mains \"including 4x 3.6 V replaceable batteries\" and renewable \"including 4x 3.6 V batteries\" — Table 2 has backup batteries in AC/DC configs too. That's a detail; I could note in config or skip. I'll add attribute like battery_backup_count for FEW45: 4. Hmm — maybe capture as model_level: auxiliary_battery_count: 4 for FEW45Y.Z, absent for others. I'll include it for completeness: values [{model FEW45Y.Z, value 4}]. Eh, might be over-engineering. I'll include it since it distinguishes the models.\n\n- Electromagnetic environment: Table 1: E2; Table 2: E1 with external antenna cable, E2 without. So model_level with config... This is both model AND config dependent. Could do model_level: attribute electromagnetic_environment, values: [{model: \"FEW41Y.Z/FEW43Y.Z\", value: \"E2\"}, {model: \"FEW45Y.Z (with external antenna cable)\", value: \"E1\"}, {model: \"FEW45Y.Z (without external antenna cable)\", value: \"E2\"}]. That encodes both. Good.\n\n- Software identification: \"See details in table 3\" / table 4 — reference, skip (fragment doesn't include tables 3/4 content).\n\nModel IDs: Since tables 1 groups FEW41Y.Z and FEW43Y.Z, I'll use model ids \"FEW41Y.Z\" and \"FEW43Y.Z\" and \"FEW45Y.Z\" in model_family, but for model_level values use group labels? Better to be consistent: use \"FEW41Y.Z/FEW43Y.Z\" as a group? The schema wants one entry per variant. Variants explicitly: FEW41Y.Z, FEW43Y.Z, FEW45Y.Z (X=1,3,5). Y and Z wildcards expand further but tables don't enumerate per Y/Z (that's tables 5-12 content, not present).\n\nI'll define model_family.models:\n- {model_id: \"FEW41Y.Z\", label: \"standard variant (X=1)\"}\n- {model_id: \"FEW43Y.Z\", label: \"advanced variant (X=3)\"}\n- {model_id: \"FEW45Y.Z\", label: \"premium variant with mobile comms (X=5)\"}\n- {model_id: \"FET4*\", label: \"flow sensor\"}? FET4* is a sensor designation. Include in type_designations and components rather than models? The certified type includes FET4. Hmm. I'll include it as a model too? It's part of the type. Maybe better in components: role \"flow sensor\", type_designations [\"FET4*\"]. And components: role \"transmitter\", types FEW41Y.Z/FEW43Y.Z/FEW45Y.Z, alternatives? Tables 1 vs 2 group them. I'll do:\n\ncomponents:\n- {role: \"flow sensor (flowtube)\", type_designations: [\"FET4*\"], alternatives: null, characteristics: {}}\n- {role: \"flow transmitter\", type_designations: [\"FEW41Y.Z\", \"FEW43Y.Z\", \"FEW45Y.Z\"], alternatives: \"OR\", characteristics: {}}\n\nHmm — is that fabrication? The certificate: \"Type: AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z and FET4*\" and tables mention \"flow sensor and flow transmitter\". FET4 = flowtube sensor; FEW = transmitter. This is a well-known ABB naming and the cert text implies it (\"for both flow sensor and flow transmitter\"). I think it's justified. But to be safe, maybe keep it lighter: role: \"transmitter\" types listed; role \"sensor\" FET4. I'll include.\n\nmodel_level entries (attribute × model):\n1. electromagnetic_environment:\n   values: [{model: \"FEW41Y.Z\", value: \"E2\"}, {model: \"FEW43Y.Z\", value: \"E2\"}, {model: \"FEW45Y.Z with external antenna cable\", value: \"E1\"}, {model: \"FEW45Y.Z without external antenna cable\", value: \"E2\"}]\n   Hmm, model values should be model ids. I'll use model: \"FEW45Y.Z (with external antenna cable)\". Acceptable.\n\n2. replaceable_battery_count: [{model FEW41Y.Z, 2}, {FEW43Y.Z, 2}, {FEW45Y.Z, 6}]\n3. battery_voltage: 3.6 V same for all — type_level.\n4. auxiliary/backup battery count for FEW45 (4x 3.6 V in AC and DC configs): model_level attribute \"integral_backup_battery_count\": [{model: \"FEW45Y.Z\", value: 4}]. The text: \"AC mains: 95–240 VAC @ 50/60 Hz including 4x 3.6 V replaceable batteries\" — so the AC/DC powered variants include 4 batteries. Attribute maybe \"backup_replaceable_battery_count\". I'll include.\n\nValues in the value objects: each model_level value: {model, value, unit_symbol, footnote_markers}. Unit at attribute level per schema example: {\"attribute\": \"qmax\", \"unit_symbol\": \"m³/h\", \"values\":[{model, value}]}. So unit_symbol goes at attribute level. Good.\n\ntype_level characteristics list:\n- measuring_principle: {value: \"Electromagnetic\", unit_symbol: null, unit_id: null}\n- accuracy_class: {value: \"1 and 2\"} — hmm string. Fine. Actually maybe use value: \"1 and 2\". Or [\"1\",\"2\"] not allowed (value must be number|obj|string). String it is.\n- environmental_class: {value: \"O (installed outdoors)\"} — I'll simplify value: \"O\". And add nothing else. Hmm. The parenthetical is meaningful (outdoor installation). I'll keep full string.\n- mechanical_class: {value: \"M1\"}\n- temperature_range_ambient: {value: {min: -25, max: 55}, unit_symbol: \"°C\"}\n- water_temperature_class: {value: \"T50\"}\n- water_temperature_range: {value: {min: 0.1, max: 50}, unit_symbol: \"°C\"}\n- maximum_admissible_pressure: {value: 1.6, unit_symbol: \"MPa\"} — and maybe alternative 16 bar. I'll add second attribute maximum_admissible_pressure_bar? Redundant. Just MPa. Hmm, or record bar as it's more common? Original lists MPa first. Keep MPa value 1.6. Maybe include note \"(16 bar)\" via a second characteristic maximum_admissible_pressure_alternative? I'll skip the duplicate.\n- orientation: {value: \"All positions (horizontal, vertical, or diagonal)\"} — lowercase? Keep original-ish: \"All positions (horizontal, vertical, or diagonal)\". Original: \"All positions (Horizontal, vertical, or diagonal)\". I'll write \"all positions (horizontal, vertical or diagonal)\". Fine.\n- reverse_flow: {value: \"sensor intended to measure reverse flow\"}\n- checking_facilities: {value: \"Type P (permanent) automatic checking facility\"}\n- power_supply_voltage_ac: {value: {min: 95, max: 240}, unit_symbol: \"V\"} — VAC; use \"V\" symbol. \n- power_supply_frequency: {value: {min: 50, max: 60}, unit_symbol: \"Hz\"}\n- power_supply_voltage_dc: {value: {min: 6, max: 32}, unit_symbol: \"V\"}\n- battery_voltage: {value: 3.6, unit_symbol: \"V\"}\n- battery_operating_voltage_range: {value: {min: 2.9, max: 3.6}, unit_symbol: \"V\"}\n- power_supply_type: AC mains / DC (renewable) / battery — could add {value: \"AC mains, DC (renewable energy), replaceable battery\"}. Optional. I'll add power_supply_type: \"AC mains or DC (renewable energy) or replaceable battery\". Hmm. Maybe skip to reduce noise. I'll include a simple one: power_supply_type: {value: \"AC mains, DC renewable energy, replaceable battery\"}. Eh. I'll skip; voltage/frequency entries suffice... Actually rule 3 example shows power_supply_type as separate char. I'll include it.\n\nconfig_level:\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class, axis \"nominal size (DN)\":\n  - {condition: \"DN < 250\", value: \"U0 and D0 (0 x DN upstream, 0 x DN downstream)\"}\n  - {condition: \"DN ≥ 250 (full bore)\", value: \"U3 and D0 (3 x DN upstream, 0 x DN downstream)\"}\n  Note: \"except for full bore sizes DN250 and larger\" — the U3 applies to full bore sizes DN250+. Condition: \"full bore sizes DN250 and larger\".\n- pressure_loss_class, axis \"bore type and nominal size (DN)\":\n  - {condition: \"reduced bore, DN40 and DN50\", value: \"Δp 40\"}\n  - {condition: \"reduced bore, sizes larger than DN50\", value: \"Δp 63\"}\n  - {condition: \"(virtual) full bore, DN40 and DN50\", value: \"Δp 10\"}\n  - {condition: \"(virtual) full bore, DN65 to DN200\", value: \"Δp 16\"}\n  - {condition: \"(virtual) full bore, DN250 and larger\", value: \"Δp 10\"}\n- maximum_pressure_loss, same axis:\n  - 0.40 bar, 0.63 bar, 0.10 bar, 0.16 bar, 0.10 bar\n\nCertificate level:\n- number: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\" — revision 11. Schema: number, scheme, project_number, page_total, member_state, date_issued, oiml_issuer_id. Revision goes to revision_history: [{revision: \"11\", date: \"27 January 2025\", changes: null}]. Hmm — the date 27 January 2025 is the issue date of this revision. revision_history entry: revision \"11\", date \"2025-01-27\". Dates: keep as given \"27 January 2025\" or ISO? I'll use ISO-ish \"2025-01-27\". The schema doesn't specify format. I'll use ISO.\n\n- scheme: \"A\"\n- project_number: \"3891281\"\n- page_total: 13\n- member_state: \"The Netherlands\"\n- date_issued: \"2025-01-27\"\n- oiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\"\n\nissuing_authority:\n- name: \"NMi Certin B.V.\" (also \"NMi Certin B.V., OIML Issuing Authority NL1\")\n- address_lines: [\"Thijsseweg 11\", \"2629 JA Delft\", \"The Netherlands\"]\n- person_responsible: \"M.Ph.D. Schmidt\"\n- person_title: null — not given. Actually \"Person responsible: M.Ph.D. Schmidt\" — no title. Certification Board signature. Omit person_title.\n- phone: \"+31 88 636 2332\"\n- email: \"certin@nmi.nl\"\n- website: \"www.nmi.nl\"\n- oiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\"\n\napplicants: [{name: \"ABB Limited\", address_lines: [\"Oldends Lane\", \"GL10 3TA Stonehouse\", \"United Kingdom\"]}]\nmanufacturers: same (Applicant and Manufacturer). So manufacturers: [{name: \"ABB Limited\", address_lines: [...]}].\n\ncertified_type:\n- category: \"Water meter\" (per example list includes \"Gas meter\"; water meter fits; R49 water meters). The doc says \"water meter\". Use \"Water meter\".\n- type_designations: [\"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\", \"FET4*\", \"FEW41Y.Z\", \"FEW43Y.Z\", \"FEW45Y.Z\"]. Hmm \"AquaMaster4\" itself is the family name. I'll include \"AquaMaster4\"? family_name covers it. type_designations: [\"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\", \"FET4*\"] plus concrete transmitter types from tables. I'll include all five plus maybe \"AquaMaster4\". Let me include: [\"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\", \"FET4*\", \"FEW41Y.Z\", \"FEW43Y.Z\", \"FEW45Y.Z\"].\n- module_designation: null / omit. Not mentioned.\n- description: \"An electromagnetic water meter\" — combine with wildcard explanation: \"Electromagnetic water meter. X being 1 for standard or 3 for advanced or 5 for premium (mobile Comms - MC) variant; Y being 1, 2, 8 or 9 (integral/remote version, optionally sensor only); Z being R (reduced bore), V (virtual full bore) or F (full bore).\" I'll put a concise description.\n\nrecommendation:\n- id: \"R49\" (canonical: \"R60\" not \"OIML R 60\"). So \"R49\"? But it's R 49-1. The canonical id could be \"R49\". Edition: 2013? The rec is \"R 49-1 (2013)\". edition: 2013. Hmm — R49-1 is part 1 of the 2012/2014 edition... The certificate says R 49-1 (2013). I'll set id: \"R49-1\", edition: 2013? Schema example: id \"R60\", edition int. For R49-1, id \"R49\" with edition 2013? The doc explicitly: \"R 49-1 (2013)\". I'll use id: \"R49-1\", edition: 2013. Hmm canonicalization example says \"R60\" not \"OIML R 60\" — strips \"OIML\" prefix. \"R49-1\" retains part suffix, that's fine. Actually maybe id should be \"R49\" and the part noted... I'll go with \"R49-1\" since that's what's printed and it's still canonical-ish. Hmm. Risky either way. Many extractor conventions: \"R49-1\". I'll keep \"R49-1\".\n- edition: 2013\n- amendment: null/omit\n- scheme: \"A\"\n- accuracy_classes: [1, 2]\n\ntest_reports: role — these are results of tests and examinations \"provided in the associated reports\" — these are Type Evaluation Report(s). The cert says \"represented by the sample(s) identified in the OIML Type Evaluation Report\". The listed reports are likely the type evaluation reports / test reports. I'll mark them role: \"type_evaluation_report\". And documentation folder T11851-8: role \"documentation_file\", id \"T11851-8\", no date/pages.\n\nReports:\n- {id: \"NMi-2254799-01\", date: \"2020-08-14\", pages: 235, role: \"type_evaluation_report\"}\n- {id: \"NMi-2254799-03\", date: \"2020-09-30\", pages: 44, role: \"type_evaluation_report\"}\n- {id: \"NMi-2254799-04\", date: \"2020-12-24\", pages: 180, role: \"type_evaluation_report\"}\n- {id: \"NMi-2564429-01\", date: \"2021-08-02\", pages: 26, role: \"type_evaluation_report\"}\n- {id: \"NMi-2496280-01\", date: \"2021-08-26\", pages: 29, role: \"type_evaluation_report\"}\n- {id: \"NMi-2496280-02\", date: \"2021-11-23\", pages: 25, role: \"type_evaluation_report\"}\n- {id: \"NMi-2563125-01\", date: \"2023-09-22\", pages: 94, role: \"type_evaluation_report\"}\n- {id: \"T11851-8\", role: \"documentation_file\"}\n\nHmm, maybe the earlier reports are TER and later are test reports for revisions — can't distinguish; use \"type_evaluation_report\" for all listed. Fine.\n\nrevision_history: [{revision: \"11\", date: \"2025-01-27\", changes: null}] — changes not stated; omit changes? Rule: omit if not present. I'll omit \"changes\" key or set null. Omit.\n\nmatrix_tables: none in this fragment (the <table> tags are label-value pairs, not matrices). Omit.\n\nfootnotes: none. Omit.\n\nNow, model_level values structure: schema example: {\"attribute\": \"qmax\", \"unit_symbol\": \"m³/h\", \"values\": [{\"model\": \"G1.6\", \"value\": 2.5}]}. Rule 2 says every characteristic value MUST be an object with value/unit_symbol/unit_id/footnote_markers shape. But the model_level example shows values as {model, value} without unit per value (unit at attribute level). I'll follow the schema example: attribute-level unit_symbol, values: [{model, value}]. For config_level, rule 5: \"Values follow the same structured form: {condition, value, unit_symbol}\". OK.\n\nShould each value be an object {value, unit_symbol, unit_id, footnote_markers} nested? The schema's model_level example: values[]: {model, value, footnote_markers[]}. So no nested unit object per value; unit at attribute level. I'll follow that.\n\nFor type_level, rule 2's strict shape: {value, unit_symbol, unit_id, footnote_markers}. I'll include unit_id: null and footnote_markers: [] or omit empty ones. I'll include unit_symbol and set unit_id null only where a unit exists? To be safe include \"unit_id\": null everywhere and omit footnote_markers when empty. Hmm, keep it consistent: include value, unit_symbol, unit_id; omit footnote_markers when empty. Actually rule 8: don't fabricate; unit_id null is fine as placeholder.\n\nLet me decide on model labels for model_family:\n\nmodels:\n- {model_id: \"FEW41Y.Z\", label: \"AquaMaster4 transmitter, standard variant (X=1)\"}\n- {model_id: \"FEW43Y.Z\", label: \"AquaMaster4 transmitter, advanced variant (X=3)\"}\n- {model_id: \"FEW45Y.Z\", label: \"AquaMaster4 transmitter, premium variant with mobile comms (X=5)\"}\n\nAnd attributes with wildcards Y, Z remain in the ids as wildcards. That's fine — the cert itself uses wildcard designations.\n\nmodel_level attributes:\n1. electromagnetic_environment: unit null. values:\n   - {model: \"FEW41Y.Z\", value: \"E2\"}\n   - {model: \"FEW43Y.Z\", value: \"E2\"}\n   - {model: \"FEW45Y.Z (with external antenna cable)\", value: \"E1\"}\n   - {model: \"FEW45Y.Z (without external antenna cable)\", value: \"E2\"}\n2. replaceable_battery_count: values 2,2,6. unit null.\n3. backup_replaceable_battery_count (included in AC and DC powered configurations): {model: \"FEW45Y.Z\", value: 4}. Name maybe \"integral_replaceable_battery_count_in_ac_dc_configurations\". Simpler: \"auxiliary_battery_count_3_6V\". Hmm. The text: AC mains \"including 4x 3.6 V replaceable batteries\"; renewable \"including 4x 3.6 V replaceable batteries\". Attribute: \"replaceable_batteries_included_in_mains_and_dc_configurations\". I'll use \"auxiliary_replaceable_battery_count\". Value 4.\n\nHmm, is including this over-fabrication? It's directly stated. Keep it.\n\nAlso water_temperature_class T50 is same both tables → type_level. All others shared.\n\nSoftware identification reference: skip.\n\nNow certificate-level characteristics? Tables 1&2 accuracy class \"1 and 2\" → covered by recommendation.accuracy_classes. I'll still include accuracy_class in type_level as \"1 and 2\"? Slight duplication; fine to include once. I'll include in type_level since it's in the characteristic tables. Value \"1 and 2\" string.\n\nPressure loss numeric + class — decided both config_level entries. Let me write them.\n\nconfig_level:\n[\n {\n  \"attribute\": \"flow_profile_sensitivity_class\",\n  \"axis\": \"nominal size (DN)\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": \"sizes smaller than DN250\", \"value\": \"U0 and D0 (0 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream)\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"full bore sizes DN250 and larger\", \"value\": \"U3 and D0 (3 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream)\"}\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"attribute\": \"pressure_loss_class\",\n  \"axis\": \"bore type and nominal size (DN)\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": \"reduced bore, DN40 and DN50\", \"value\": \"Δp 40\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"reduced bore, sizes larger than DN50\", \"value\": \"Δp 63\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"virtual or full bore, DN40 and DN50\", \"value\": \"Δp 10\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"virtual or full bore, DN65 to DN200\", \"value\": \"Δp 16\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"virtual or full bore, DN250 and larger\", \"value\": \"Δp 10\"}\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"attribute\": \"maximum_pressure_loss\",\n  \"axis\": \"bore type and nominal size (DN)\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": \"reduced bore, DN40 and DN50\", \"value\": 0.4, \"unit_symbol\": \"bar\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"reduced bore, sizes larger than DN50\", \"value\": 0.63, \"unit_symbol\": \"bar\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"virtual or full bore, DN40 and DN50\", \"value\": 0.1, \"unit_symbol\": \"bar\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"virtual or full bore, DN65 to DN200\", \"value\": 0.16, \"unit_symbol\": \"bar\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"virtual or full bore, DN250 and larger\", \"value\": 0.1, \"unit_symbol\": \"bar\"}\n  ]\n }\n]\n\nWait — \"Virtual full bore\" is V; \"Full bore\" is F. The pressure loss cell says \"(Virtual) Full bore\" — grouping V and F together. So condition \"virtual or full bore\" is right.\n\nDecimal fix: \"0,40\" → 0.4, \"0,63\" → 0.63, \"0,10\" → 0.1, \"0,16\" → 0.16. Good.\n\nNote that the U0/U3 flow profile sensitivity applies to both tables 1 and 2 identically → type-level config (axis = size), fine.\n\nMAP: 1.6 MPa (16 bar). value 1.6 MPa. Both tables same → type_level.\n\nTemperature ambient: -25/+55 °C.\n\nBattery: Table 1: \"Replaceable Battery: 2x 3,6 V (operating range: 2,9 - 3,6 V)\". Table 2: 6x. battery_voltage type_level 3.6 V; battery_operating_voltage_range {2.9-3.6} V type_level; battery count model_level.\n\nPower supply type: AC mains / renewable energy (DC) / replaceable battery. I'll add \"power_supply_type\": {value: \"AC mains, DC renewable energy, or replaceable battery\"}. Hmm... I'll phrase: \"AC mains, renewable energy (DC powered), or replaceable battery\".\n\nOrientation: value \"All positions (horizontal, vertical, or diagonal)\".\n\nReverse flow: value \"sensor intended to measure reverse flow\" — maybe better attribute reverse_flow: value \"measured\". I'll keep faithful string: \"The sensor is intended to measure reverse flow\" — trim to \"intended to measure reverse flow\"? Just keep original text minus period.\n\nChecking facilities: \"Type P (permanent) automatic checking facility\".\n\nEnvironmental class: \"O (installed outdoors)\".\n\nDescription: \"An electromagnetic water meter. X: 1 = standard, 3 = advanced, 5 = premium (mobile Comms - MC) variant. Y: 1, 2, 8 or 9 (integral/remote version, optionally sensor only). Z: R = reduced bore, V = virtual full bore, F = full bore.\"\n\ncertificate.number: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\". \n\nIssuing authority name: \"NMi Certin B.V.\" — full: \"NMi Certin B.V., OIML Issuing Authority NL1\". I'll use name \"NMi Certin B.V.\" and oiml_issuer_id \"NL1\".\n\nDate issued: 27 January 2025 → \"2025-01-27\".\n\noiml_issuer_id at certificate level: \"NL1\".\n\nPerson responsible: \"M.Ph.D. Schmidt\". Keep as-is.\n\nAlso \"Certification Board\" — no extra person. Skip.\n\nNow assemble JSON. Keep it parseable. No fences per rules... The instruction says return only JSON, no markdown fences. I'll output raw JSON.\n\nDouble-check numbers: 95–240 VAC: min 95 max 240. 50/60 Hz: {50,60}. 6–32 V: {6,32}. 3,6 V → 3.6. 2,9 - 3,6 → {2.9, 3.6}. -25/+55 °C → {-25, 55}. +0,1/+50 → {0.1, 50}. 1,6 MPa → 1.6.\n\nmodel_family.family_name: \"AquaMaster4\".\n\nShould FET4* be a model? It's the sensor type; I'll leave it in type_designations and components.\n\nComponents: \n[\n  {\"role\": \"flow transmitter\", \"type_designations\": [\"FEW41Y.Z\", \"FEW43Y.Z\", \"FEW45Y.Z\"], \"alternatives\": \"OR\", \"characteristics\": {}},\n  {\"role\": \"flow sensor\", \"type_designations\": [\"FET4*\"], \"alternatives\": null, \"characteristics\": {}}\n]\n\nHmm \"alternatives\": for transmitter types — the cert offers FEW41, FEW43, FEW45 as variants — they're distinct models rather than alternatives; but \"OR\" fits (\"types ... and ...\"). I'll set \"OR\" for transmitter group. For FET sensor, alternatives null — omit. Schema shows alternatives (\"OR\"|\"AND\"|null). I'll set null.\n\nAlso \"indicating device\" is named in tables 9-12 titles — a component (indicating device / calculator). But its characteristics (tables 9-12) aren't in this fragment. Should I include components entry with role \"indicating device\"? The titles on page 2 mention it. It's part of the instrument family. Including a component with no type designations seems thin. I'll skip it — tables 5-12 not present. Actually the mention \"indicating device\" in table list implies the instrument includes one; but without type designations, skip. Keep components to transmitter + sensor. Hmm, even those: the type line \"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z and FET4*\" — FET4 is the sensor. I'm fairly confident. Keep.\n\ncharacteristics.type_level entries list (both tables share):\n- measuring_principle: \"Electromagnetic\"\n- accuracy_class: \"1 and 2\"\n- environmental_class: \"O (installed outdoors)\"\n- mechanical_class: \"M1\"\n- temperature_range_ambient: {-25,55} °C\n- water_temperature_class: \"T50\"\n- water_temperature_range: {0.1,50} °C\n- maximum_admissible_pressure: 1.6 MPa\n- orientation: \"all positions (horizontal, vertical, or diagonal)\"\n- reverse_flow: \"sensor intended to measure reverse flow\"\n- checking_facilities: \"Type P (permanent) automatic checking facility\"\n- power_supply_type: \"AC mains, renewable energy (DC powered), or replaceable battery\"\n- power_supply_voltage_ac: {95,240} V\n- power_supply_frequency: {50,60} Hz\n- power_supply_voltage_dc: {6,32} V\n- battery_voltage: 3.6 V\n- battery_operating_voltage_range: {2.9,3.6} V\n\nunit_id: null for all (rule: leave null unless certain). °C → \"u:degree_Celsius\" is given as example of a valid ID; but rule says leave null unless certain. The example explicitly shows \"u:degree_Celsius\" as valid. I could fill for °C, V, Hz, MPa? Not certain of exact unitsdb IDs beyond the example. I'll set unit_id null everywhere except maybe degree_Celsius... The example in rule 2 lists \"u:degree_Celsius\" as a valid value. I'll set unit_id \"u:degree_Celsius\" for °C entries, null otherwise. Safe.\n\nNow finalize model_level:\n\n[\n {\"attribute\": \"electromagnetic_environment\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"values\": [\n   {\"model\": \"FEW41Y.Z\", \"value\": \"E2\"},\n   {\"model\": \"FEW43Y.Z\", \"value\": \"E2\"},\n   {\"model\": \"FEW45Y.Z (with external antenna cable)\", \"value\": \"E1\"},\n   {\"model\": \"FEW45Y.Z (without external antenna cable)\", \"value\": \"E2\"}\n ]},\n {\"attribute\": \"replaceable_battery_count\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"values\": [\n   {\"model\": \"FEW41Y.Z\", \"value\": 2},\n   {\"model\": \"FEW43Y.Z\", \"value\": 2},\n   {\"model\": \"FEW45Y.Z\", \"value\": 6}\n ]},\n {\"attribute\": \"auxiliary_replaceable_battery_count\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"values\": [\n   {\"model\": \"FEW45Y.Z\", \"value\": 4}\n ]}\n]\n\nMaybe describe auxiliary attribute: it's batteries included in AC-mains and renewable-DC powered configurations of FEW45Y.Z. Attribute name: \"replaceable_batteries_included_in_ac_and_dc_configurations\". A bit long but descriptive. I'll use that.\n\nOutput JSON structure:\n\n{\n  \"certificate\": {\n    \"number\": \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\",\n    \"scheme\": \"A\",\n    \"project_number\": \"3891281\",\n    \"page_total\": 13,\n    \"member_state\": \"The Netherlands\",\n    \"date_issued\": \"2025-01-27\",\n    \"oiml_issuer_id\": \"NL1\"\n  },\n  \"issuing_authority\": {\n    \"name\": \"NMi Certin B.V.\",\n    \"address_lines\": [\"Thijsseweg 11\", \"2629 JA Delft\", \"The Netherlands\"],\n    \"person_responsible\": \"M.Ph.D. Schmidt\",\n    \"phone\": \"+31 88 636 2332\",\n    \"email\": \"certin@nmi.nl\",\n    \"website\": \"www.nmi.nl\",\n    \"oiml_issuer_id\": \"NL1\"\n  },\n  \"applicants\": [{\"name\": \"ABB Limited\", \"address_lines\": [\"Oldends Lane\", \"GL10 3TA Stonehouse\", \"United Kingdom\"]}],\n  \"manufacturers\": [{\"name\": \"ABB Limited\", \"address_lines\": [\"Oldends Lane\", \"GL10 3TA Stonehouse\", \"United Kingdom\"]}],\n  \"certified_type\": {\n    \"category\": \"Water meter\",\n    \"type_designations\": [\"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\", \"FET4*\", \"FEW41Y.Z\", \"FEW43Y.Z\", \"FEW45Y.Z\"],\n    \"module_designation\": null,\n    \"description\": \"An electromagnetic water meter. X = 1 (standard), 3 (advanced) or 5 (premium, mobile Comms - MC) variant; Y = 1, 2, 8 or 9 (integral/remote version, optionally sensor only); Z = R (reduced bore), V (virtual full bore) or F (full bore).\"\n  },\n  \"model_family\": {\n    \"family_name\": \"AquaMaster4\",\n    \"models\": [\n      {\"model_id\": \"FEW41Y.Z\", \"label\": \"standard variant (X=1)\"},\n      {\"model_id\": \"FEW43Y.Z\", \"label\": \"advanced variant (X=3)\"},\n      {\"model_id\": \"FEW45Y.Z\", \"label\": \"premium variant with mobile comms (X=5)\"}\n    ]\n  },\n  \"components\": [...],\n  \"characteristics\": {\n    \"type_level\": {...},\n    \"model_level\": [...],\n    \"config_level\": [...]\n  },\n  \"recommendation\": {\n    \"id\": \"R49-1\",\n    \"edition\": 2013,\n    \"scheme\": \"A\",\n    \"accuracy_classes\": [1, 2]\n  },\n  \"test_reports\": [...],\n  \"revision_history\": [{\"revision\": \"11\", \"date\": \"2025-01-27\"}]\n}\n\nWait: type_level is an object of label→{value, unit_symbol, unit_id}. Per schema: \"type_level: <label>: {value, unit, footnote_markers[]}\". Example shows keys mapping to value objects. Good.\n\nShould I include footnote_markers only when present — yes omit empty.\n\naccuracy_classes: values as strings \"1\" and \"2\" or ints [1,2]. Use [1,2].\n\nOne more check: certificate date — the signing date \"27 January 2025\" appears near Issuing Authority block: \"NMi Certin B.V., OIML Issuing Authority NL1, 27 January 2025, Certification Board\". That's the issue date of the certificate (revision 11). Use as date_issued.\n\nTest report roles: The intro says \"The conformity was established by the results of tests and examinations provided in the associated reports\". These are Type Evaluation Reports presumably. Given role enum, I'll use \"type_evaluation_report\" for the seven NMi reports and \"documentation_file\" for T11851-8.\n\nPages as ints.\n\nDates ISO: \n- 14 August 2020 → 2020-08-14\n- 30 September 2020 → 2020-09-30\n- 24 December 2020 → 2020-12-24\n- 2 August 2021 → 2021-08-02\n- 26 August 2021 → 2021-08-26\n- 23 November 2021 → 2021-11-23\n- 22 September 2023 → 2023-09-22\n\nDocumentation file: {\"id\": \"T11851-8\", \"role\": \"documentation_file\"} — description mentions \"Documentation folder no. T11851-8\".\n\nI think that's complete. Write final JSON only.",
